  \dt{Reply-to:}        \dd{Jan Schultke <\mail{janschultke@gmail.com}>\br
                            Matt Borland <\mail{matt@mattborland.com}>}
  \dt{GitHub Issue:}    \dd{\ref("https://wg21.link/P4355/github")}
  \dt{Source:}          \dd{\ref("https://github.com/eisenwave/cpp-proposals/blob/main/src/float-saturating-cast.cow")}
}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
\tcode{std::saturating_cast} should accept floating-point operands.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

C++ is currently an outlier among programming languages
in terms of the conversion between floating-point types and integer types.
The behavior is specified in \eelis{conv.fpint#1}:

\Bquote{
A prvalue of a floating-point type can be converted to a prvalue of an integer type.
The conversion truncates; that is, the fractional part is discarded.
The behavior is undefined\iref{ub:conv.fpint.float.not.represented}
if the truncated value cannot be represented in the destination type.
}

Notably, any infinities and NaNs result in UB,
and any value outside the representable range
(possibly as the result of some numerical instability) results in UB.

\h3{Hardware support}

The status quo actually makes sense because it allows for high performance on
a variety of architectures.
Mainstream architectures typically have an instruction for converting
floating-point numbers to integers.
Let's focus on the conversion from \tcode{float32_t} to \tcode{int32_t}.

\style{
.val-table td {
  text-align: right;
}

.center {
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
}
}

\table(class="val-table center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Input}
    \th{\ref("https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/cvttss2si"){\tt{CVTTSS2SI}}\br (x86_64)}
    \th{\ref("https://docs.riscv.org/reference/isa/v20260120/unpriv/f-st-ext.html#20-1-7-single-precision-floating-point-conversion-and-move-instructions"){\tt{FCVT.W.S}}\br (RISC-V)}
    \th{\ref("https://support.arm.com/documentation/dui0801/h/A64-Floating-point-Instructions/FCVTZS--scalar--integer-"){\tt{FCVTZS}}\br (AArch64)}
    \th{\ref("https://webassembly.github.io/spec/core/exec/numerics.html#xref-exec-numerics-op-trunc-sat-mathrm-trunc-sat-s-m-n-z"){\tt{i32.trunc_sat_s_f32}}\br (WASM)}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{small \math{\mi{x}}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{NaN}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{0}}
    \td{\tcode{0}}
  }
}

Because everything but the handling of small values which are actually representable
as the resulting integer is UB,
the \tcode{float → int} conversion can always be lowered to a single instruction.

\Bnote{
The \tcode{INT32_MIN} results on x86_64 are just an error indicator.
The IA flag is also raised, indicating an invalid instruction.

For RISC-V, the assumption is that the \tcode{RTZ} (round to zero)
rounding mode was explicitly specified.
}

\h3{Comparison with other languages}

Similar to comparing hardware capabilities,
we can also compare how other languages handle this conversion:

\table(class="val-table center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Input}
    \th{\ref("https://eel.is/c++draft/conv.fpint"){C++}}
    \th{\ref("https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/operator-expr.html#numeric-cast"){Rust}}
    \th{\ref("https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se24/html/jls-5.html#jls-5.1.3"){Java}}
    \th{\ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/language-specification/conversions#1032-explicit-numeric-conversions"){C# (checked)}}
    \th{\ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/language-specification/conversions#1032-explicit-numeric-conversions"){C# (unchecked)}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{small \math{\mi{x}}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{NaN}}}
    \td{UB}
