    \td{\tcode{0}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
}

The key insight is that the saturating behavior is considered so useful
that it is baked into the core language of Rust and Java.

\Bnote{
When specifying
\ref("https://clang.llvm.org/docs/UsersManual.html#cmdoption-f-no-strict-float-cast-overflow"){\tt{-fno-strict-float-cast-overflow}},
Clang's behavior matches that of Rust and Java.
}

\h3{The problem}

The problem is that making almost every part of the tables above undefined behavior
is not very useful for some domains.
It is fairly common that when quantizing floating-point values to integers,
some values are outside the representable range,
especially in the domain of signal processing.

In that domain, saturating behavior would be much more useful.
Frustratingly, we have a \tcode{std::saturating_cast} function,
but that function is only defined for integer types.

\h3{Proposal}

The way to solve the problem would be to extend
\tcode{std::saturating_cast} to also support floating-point types.

It would also be possible to make changes to the core language,
aligning C++ with Rust and Java,
but this would have considerable performance cost
which massive amounts of existing code would silently incur.
Clang users would all have to start using \tt{-fstrict-float-cast-overflow}
to restore the old behavior,
but this flag would no longer be a compliant extension because it would introduced UB
where the standard defines behavior.

\h2{Motivation}

We already see some evidence that a saturating cast from floating-point to integer is useful.
This is the behavior of the core-language conversion in Rust and Java,
and AAarch64 has an instruction which implements this behavior exactly.

In C++, one can similarly imagine many applications for this behavior:

\h3{Quantization}

It is common in calculations related to RGB colors to represent the linearized
color values as floating-point numbers in the range [0, 1].
When stored as pixel values,
they are typically quantized to 8-bit integers.
It is also common for some values to fall outside the [0, 1] range
due to numerical instability or because the original color space had a wider gamut.
That makes the UB in the conversion from \tcode{float} to \tcode{std::uint8_t} a safety hazard.
While the problem can be solved by clamping to the range [0, 1] before the conversion,
this is pessimistic because on e.g. ARM,
the underlying \tt{FCVTZU} instruction already clamps
(saturating has a clamping effect).

\h4{Optimal output using Rust}

Looking at
\ref("https://godbolt.org/z/6a6z4h1rf"){\tt{rustc} output}
lets us compare to the theoretical optimum:
\codeblock("rust"){
pub fn clamped(x: f32) -> u8 {
    return (x.clamp(0.0, 1.0) * 255f32) as u8;
}
pub fn unclamped(x: f32) -> u8 {
    return (x * 255f32) as u8;
}
}

\codeblock("txt"){
example[ceccecd8beb92274]::clamped:
        movi    d1, #0000000000000000
        fcmp    s0, #0.0
        mov     w8, #1132396544
        mov     w9, #255
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, mi
        fmov    s1, #1.00000000
        fcmp    s0, s1
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, gt
        fmov    s1, w8
        fmul    s0, s0, s1
        fcvtzu  w8, s0
        cmp     w8, #255
        csel    w0, w8, w9, lo
        ret

example[ceccecd8beb92274]::unclamped:
        mov     w8, #1132396544
        mov     w9, #255
        fmov    s1, w8
        fmul    s0, s0, s1
        fcvtzu  w8, s0
        cmp     w8, #255
        csel    w0, w8, w9, lo
        ret
}
The output for \tcode{unclamped} shows that \code("rust"){f32 as u8}
was simply lowered to a \tt{fcvtzu} instruction,
then clamped to the range [0, 255] via \tt{cmp} and \tt{csel}.
This is the theoretical optimum we would expect.

\h4{Low-quality output using C++}

C++ developers are essentially forced to get the \tcode{clamped} output
(see \url{https://godbolt.org/z/dqofdPW7a})
because without clamping,
they would run into UB for infinities and large values:
\cppblock{
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>
#include <numeric>
std::uint8_t clamped(float x) {
    return std::saturating_cast<std::uint8_t>(unsigned(std::clamp(x, 0.f, 1.f) * 255));
}
}
\codeblock("txt"){
clamped(float):
        movi    d1, #0000000000000000
        fcmp    s0, #0.0
        mov     w8, #1132396544
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, mi
        fmov    s1, #1.00000000
        fcmp    s0, s1
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, gt
        fmov    s1, w8
        fmul    s0, s0, s1
        fcvtzu  w8, s0
        cmp     w8, #256
        csinv   w0, w8, wzr, lo
        ret
}

The frustrating part is that the \q{theoretical optimum} on ARM is known,
but there is no way to express it in the C++ language without UB.
Getting to the optimal compiler output simply through optimizations
also seems unrealistic.
If \tcode{std::saturating_cast} accepted floating-point types,
we could go straight from \tcode{float} to \tcode{std::uint8_t},
producing the same output as Rust's \code("rust"){f32 as u8} conversion.

\h3{Safety}

Another obvious problem with a language facility that has so much UB is safety.
If someone wanted to avoid core language UB,
\tcode{float → int} conversions would present a minefield,
and there is no clear alternative.

\tcode{std::saturating_cast} is that safer alternative:
whenever a C++ developer is unsure whether a \tcode{float → int} conversion is safe,
they can use \tcode{std::saturating_cast} to avoid UB.
For the conversions that are already well-defined,
this results in no change in behavior,
and on platforms like AAarch64 or WASM,
it results in no performance cost either.

\h3{Engine implementation}

The saturating behavior is gradually becoming the de-facto standard for how to handle
conversions from floating-point to integer types.
Languages like Rust and Java handle conversions like this and
architectures like AAarch64 and WASM have instructions for this behavior.
That makes it increasingly relevant for C++ to have such an operation as well,
to make the implementation of compilers,
WASM runtimes,
emulators,
etc. easier and more efficient.

\h2{Design}

\h3{Strategy}

The overall strategy for \tcode{std::saturating_cast}
is to match the Rust and Java behavior,
as well as the \tt{FCVTZS} and \tt{i32.trunc_sat_s_f32} instructions.

\h3{Handling of NaN}

The proposed behavior is to return zero for NaN inputs,
which matches the aforementioned precedent.

One could also match the RISC-V \tt{FCVT.W.S} behavior
of returning \tt{INT32_MAX} for NaN inputs,
but there is a real benefit to keeping the result for NaN inputs distinct:
If \tcode{std::isfinite(x)} is \tcode{false} for the original input,
the result of the saturating cast also provides information about which
non-finite value was passed in
(\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\mi{∞}}}, \math{\mi{+}\mi{∞}}, or \math{\mi{NaN}}).

\h3{Floating-point exceptions and constant expressions}

The handling of floating-point exceptions should be
consistent with the rest of the standard library,
such as any functions in \header{cmath},
and should try to be consistent with the \tt{FCVTZS} instruction.
The Arm Architecture Reference Manual Armv8 specifies:

\Bquote{
\b{Invalid Operation}

Occurs if the floating-point input is a NaN,
infinity, or a numerical value that cannot be represented
in the destination register.
An out of range integer or fixed-point result is saturated to the size of the
destination register.

\b{Inexact}

Occurs if the numeric result that differs from the input value.
}

Curiously, C's Annex F leaves it unspecified whether a \tcode{float → int}
conversion raises the \q{inexact} exception,
so out of caution and to grant some implementation freedom,
we should not mandate it either.

A domain error should only take if the input is NaN.
Otherwise, \tcode{std::saturating_cast} is quasi-useless during constant evaluation.
\tcode{static_cast} and \tcode{std::saturating_cast} would be equivalent in that case
because everything that is UB in \tcode{static_cast}
turns into a domain error in \tcode{std::saturating_cast},
and domain errors disqualify expressions from being constant expressions in the standard library.
It also doesn't make any design sense for an operation that is explicitly saturating
to report a domain error when it saturates.

\h3{Other saturating operations}

The rest of the saturation arithmetic library is not affected.
That is, there is no floating-point support for \tcode{std::saturating_add} etc.
These functions are simply not in scope for the paper,
and the motivation does not apply to them because floating-point operations
are already saturating
(in the sense that they clamp to \math{\mo{−}\mi{∞}} and \math{\mi{+}\mi{∞}}).

I also don't propose floating-point types as a result of \tcode{std::saturating_cast}
because converting to floating-point types is already quasi-saturating.

\h2{Impact on existing code}

The proposed change only relaxes the constraints on
\tcode{std::saturating_cast} to allow floating-point types.
Existing uses with integer operands are not affected.

\h2{Implementation experience}

The proposed behavior is available in LLVM as the
\ref("https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#llvm-fptoui-sat-intrinsic"){\tt{llvm.fptoui.sat.*}} and
\ref("https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#llvm-fptosi-sat-intrinsic"){\tt{llvm.fptosi.sat.*}}
family of intrinsics.
However, these are not yet exposed as a standalone Clang intrinsic.
To emit them, one must specify
\ref("https://clang.llvm.org/docs/UsersManual.html#cmdoption-f-no-strict-float-cast-overflow"){\tt{-fno-strict-float-cast-overflow}},
and that doesn't have any effect on constant evaluation,
so infinities and NaNs need explicit handling at compile time.

\h3{Possible implementation}

A pure library implementation with some \tcode{if} statements for range checks
is theoretically possible, but a waste of time from an implementer viewpoint.
The goal is to emit exactly one e.g. \tt{FCVTZS}
instruction for the conversion
(or whatever the minimal equivalent is on other architectures),
and this requires compiler intrinsics.
Nonetheless, a library implementation looks as follows:

\cppblock{
#include <limits>
#include <cmath>
#include <bit>

template<class R, class F>
constexpr R saturating_cast(F x) noexcept {
  if (std::isnan(x)) {
    return R(0);
  }

  if constexpr (std::numeric_limits<R>::digits >= std::numeric_limits<F>::max_exponent) {
    // Every truncated finite number is representable as an integer of type R,
    // so we don't have to worry about numbers with large magnitude when casting.
    // Infinities and NaNs are the only non-representable values.
    //
    // In practice, this happens in e.g. float32_t → unsigned __int128
    if (std::isinf(x)) {
      return x < F(0) ? std::numeric_limits<R>::lowest()
                      : std::numeric_limits<R>::max();
    }
  } else if (std::is_signed_v<R>) {
    // Otherwise, we need to compute the bounds of the range (min, max)
    // (i.e. exclusive on both ends).
    // that does not cause casting to have undefined behavior.
    // Infinities also fall outside that range and need no explicit handling.

    // Since integers use two's complement,
    // this is one past the greatest integer,
    // so already the bound we are looking for.
    constexpr F max = -F(std::numeric_limits<R>::lowest());
    if (x >= max) {
      return std::numeric_limits<R>::max();
    }

    // We use lowest() because it is a power of two,
    // so there is no risk of rounding.
    // It can only overflow to infinity.
    constexpr F min = F(std::numeric_limits<R>::lowest());

    if constexpr (min - 1 < min) {
      // Numbers within 1 distance of lowest() get truncated to lowest(),
      // so lowest() is not the bound; the next lower integer is the bound.
      if (x <= min - 1) {
        return std::numeric_limits<R>::lowest();
      }
    } else if (x < min) {
      // In this case, there exists no integer directly below lowest()
      // due to limited floating-point precision.
      // This makes lowest() a genuine exclusive bound.
      return std::numeric_limits<R>::lowest();
    }
  } else {
    // We use bit_floor to get a power of two, avoiding rounding issues.
    // Multiplication by two then gets us one past the greatest integer.
    constexpr F max = F(std::bit_floor(std::numeric_limits<R>::max())) * 2;
    if (x >= max) {
      return std::numeric_limits<R>::max();
    }
    // Since the conversion truncates, negative values greater than -1 result in 0.
    // Cast UB only happens if the truncated value is not representable in the result.
    if (x <= F(-1)) {
      return std::numeric_limits<R>::lowest();
    }
  }

  return R(x);
}
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

In \eelis{version.syn}, bump the \tcode{__cpp_lib_saturation_arithmetic} feature-test macro.

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_lib_saturation_arithmetic \del{202603L} \ins{20XXXXL} // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<numeric>}}
}
}

Change \eelis{numeric.sat.cast} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
template<class R, class T>
  constexpr R saturating_cast(T x) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
\constraints
\tcode{R} \del{and \tcode{T} are} \ins{is a}
signed or unsigned integer type\del{s}\iref{basic.fundamental}.
\ins{\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type or
a cv-unqualified floating-point type.}

\returns
\ins{If \tcode{x} is NaN, zero.
Otherwise, let \math{\mi{v}} be the value of \tcode{x}
with the fractional part (if any) discarded.}
If \del{\tcode{x}} \ins{\math{\mi{v}}}
is representable as a value of type \tcode{R},
\ins{return} \del{\tcode{x}} \ins{\math{\mi{v}}};
otherwise, \ins{returns} either the largest or smallest representable value of type \tcode{R},
whichever is closer to
\del{the value of \tcode{x}} \ins{\math{\mi{v}}}.

\ins{\remarks
If and only if \tcode{T} is is a floating-point type,
floating-point exceptions may be raised,
unless the value of \tcode{x} is exactly representable in type \tcode{R}.
Whether the \q{inexact} exception is raised is a property of the implementation.
A domain error occurs if \tcode{x} is NaN,
in which case a function call expression is not a constant expression.
}
}

\ins{SEE ALSO: ISO/IEC 9899:2024 7.12.1}
}

